[17592186486308 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486308 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486308 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486308 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486308 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486308 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ac0df0bc-bf6e-51c0-bdb6-19b9ad7ef142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486385 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486385 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486385 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486385 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3xy\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486385 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5787fa90-c666-5c53-9045-790fb21cdab8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486385 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ and $v(x,y)=3xy.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =3y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ 3y & 3x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 3x-(-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}})\\cdot 3y \\\\  & =\\frac{9x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =9u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9u}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486477 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b56f991-f12d-5895-8d7a-a6f922386e67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486477 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186281361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486480 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186486481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486480 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4da312bf-59b4-5062-8ec0-dbcdcb029a0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486480 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486481 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186486492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486481 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f3a2d34-0995-5368-905a-f5f4b05fda88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486481 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486492 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186486494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486492 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85fa7b96-b748-5bcb-8112-da4536f9e0d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486492 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186383522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486494 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186486477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486494 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8799c879-5fd1-5dbd-8c44-d6fe6